  "body": "## What changed\n\n- New MCP tool `edit_user_holder` in `crates/app/src/mcp/holder_shape_tools.rs`. It edits a holder of the document's component table by its shape fields: `taper`, `clamp`, `bore`, `gauge_len`, `nose_d`, `largest_d` and `name`. Only `id` is required.\n- The tool reads the held shape with `HolderShape::of`. It puts the stated fields over that shape. It builds the kind and the outline with `HolderShape::kind` and `HolderShape::geometry(ADAPTIVE_PORT)`, as the holder form's Apply does. Then it runs one `EditDocumentComponent` through the History. This is the same undoable command as the GUI. The existing MCP `undo` and `redo` invert it.\n- The refusals are the same as in the form:\n  - a value that cannot describe steel: `holder not changed: <ShapeError text>`;\n  - a broken fit: the command's refusal, which names the component and the tool;\n  - an empty name: `the holder needs a name`;\n  - an edit that changes nothing: the command's \"already so\".\n- The tool also refuses an id that the document does not hold, an unknown taper or clamp, and a holder whose outline is not a shape outline. The last refusal points to `upsert_tool_component`.\n- An ER or Weldon clamp without a stated `largest_d` takes `nose_d`, because these clamps have one body diameter.\n- Registration: one `.chain` line in `component_tools::all()` and one `mod` line in `mcp/mod.rs`.\n- The generated reference `docs/agents/mcp-tools.md` and the guide `site/content/guide/automate/mcp.md` name the tool (the `mcp_doc_drift` tests require this).\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/4391-mcp-edit-user-holder.md`.\n\n## The camcraft-mcp skill row\n\nThe skill is a process file. This PR does not change it. Issue #4432 (`P5`, `effort:low`) holds the proposed row, word for word.\n\n## Pinning tests\n\nNew file `crates/app/tests/tool_library_holders/holder_shape_mcp.rs`, in the existing `tool_library_holders` directory suite (no new test binary):\n\n- `mcp_edits_a_user_holder_by_its_shape_in_one_undo_step`: a change, its outline (equal to `HolderShape::geometry` of the stated shape), the tool derived again (gauge length +10 mm), one undo, one redo, and an unstated field that keeps its value.\n- `mcp_edit_user_holder_refuses_by_name_and_changes_nothing`: the `ShapeError` refusals, the broken fit (names the component and the tool), a holder that is not a shape outline, an unknown id, clamp, taper and argument, an empty name, an edit that changes nothing. After these, the holder row and the tool are unchanged.\n\n## Gate (final head `f5b1eccd0`)\n\nLog `gate-4391-f5b1eccd0.log` (first line `head f5b1eccd0a873141c9f7b423e423a8eeb7ce567e`), one lanes run on mac-1:\n\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`: 169 steps, 0 failed, 0 stalled (24.5 min). The step `app suites: tool_library_holders` ran the whole suite, with both pinning tests: ok.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test mcp_doc_drift`: 9 passed, 0 failed.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\nThe first head (`891163f75`) had one red pinning test: the fit-refusal call did not state `largest_d` (see \"Not exercised\"). Commit `f5b1eccd0` corrects the test call. The tool code did not change.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The GUI. The holder form is not changed.\n- No post, toolpath or geometry code is changed. No example catalog run and no light lane run: nothing under them changed.\n- `HolderShape::of` reads back the largest diameter of the test holder as Ø38.748558 mm, not the Ø44 mm that built it (the body is under the flange envelope). The outline is the same, so the read-back is correct. A call that changes the nose diameter on a shrink holder should state `largest_d`.\n\nChangelog: MCP: `edit_user_holder` edits a user holder by its shape fields (taper, clamp, bore, gauge length, nose and largest diameter) in one undo step, as the holder form does.\n\nEffort: medium (not escalated)\n\nFixes #4391\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
